<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plovdiv</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Philippopolis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plovdiv is the second-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> river in the Upper Thracian Plain. Inhabited since prehistoric times, it was the Thracian Eumolpias, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a>'s Philippopolis and the Roman capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, with a well-preserved Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. From 1878 to 1885 it was the capital of Eastern Rumelia, and the coup there in September 1885 brought union with Bulgaria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plovdiv.jpg" alt="Plovdiv" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Община Пловдив · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%B2%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%B2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Evidence of habitation in the area goes back to the 6th millennium BCE, when the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements appeared, and the cultural layers in the city are twelve metres thick. A Thracian settlement named Pulpudeva was later conquered and ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, Macedonians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> and Ottoman Turks. Philip II of Macedon settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> and 2,000 Macedonians there in 342 BCE and founded it as Philippopolis. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> took it in 168 BCE, and it became the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracia">Thracia</a>, at the centre of the road network of inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. Marcus Aurelius built a new wall around the city.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> under Cniva sacked Philippopolis in 250, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> did so in 441 or 442; the walls were rebuilt each time, and Justinian renewed them again. The city fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> in 863, in the reign of Boris I. Basil II used it as a strategic fortress in the wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> occupied it briefly around 1090. Geoffrey of Villehardouin ranked it the third largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. Kaloyan of Bulgaria destroyed it in 1206, and in 1219 it became the capital of the Crusader Duchy of Philippopolis.</p><p>The Ottoman Turks under Lala Shahin Pasha took the city in 1364, though other accounts place its fall after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> in 1371. Over about five hundred years it was a sanjak centre at various times, one of the major economic centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edirne">Edirne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, and its richer citizens built the houses that still stand in the architectural reserve of Old Plovdiv.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-stefano">Treaty of San Stefano</a> of 3 March 1878 chose Plovdiv as capital of a restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> separated the autonomous region of Eastern Rumelia from Bulgaria, and Plovdiv became its capital under a constitution made by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. In 1885 Eastern Rumelia joined Bulgaria. A railway linked the city with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1874 and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> in 1888, and in 1892 it hosted the First Bulgarian Fair.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Plovdiv is the second largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritsa">Maritsa</a> river 144 km southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, spread over seven syenite hills that give it the name City of the Seven Hills. It had a population of 329,489 in 2024. It was the European Capital of Culture in 2019, and the city has more than 200 archaeological sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Plovdiv” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 342 and 340 BCE he defeated Cersobleptes, imposed a tithe on the Thracians and founded settlements such as <b>Philippopolis</b> (Plovdiv) and Cabyle.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its constitution, the Organic Statute, was drafted by an international commission, and <b>Plovdiv</b> became its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-rumelia">Eastern Rumelia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Paulicians were scattered; many were resettled in the Balkans around <b>Philippopolis</b>, where their ideas fed the later Bogomil movement.</blockquote><a href="/state/paulician-state">Paulician state</a></li></ul></section>
